"machine even when the backend is remote — "
":class:`~ABQflow.DatExtractionStrategy` fetches the ``.dat`` home first, "
"nothing is uploaded, artifact paths are not remapped, and sidecar CSVs "
"are written straight into the local job directory.  Set "
"``ABQFLOW_HOST_PYTHON`` to override the interpreter when "
"``sys.executable`` is a frozen or embedded binary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:127
msgid "Resource Planning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:129
msgid ""
"The framework automatically caps parallelism to avoid oversubscribing "
"Abaqus license tokens, since a job that cannot obtain a license will "
"simply fail to start. CPU cores are not hard-capped — small jobs rarely "
"saturate a full core, so requesting more parallel jobs than physical "
"cores support (CPU oversubscription) is allowed, but it is flagged with a"
" warning so the allocation stays visible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:136
msgid ""
"**Abaqus license token formula** (official): a job using *n* CPU cores "
"consumes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:138
#, python-brace-format
msgid "T(n) = \\lceil 5 \\cdot n^{0.422} \\rceil"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:142
msgid "Example token counts: 1→5, 2→7, 4→9, 8→12, 16→16."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:144
msgid "**Parallelism limits:**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:146
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"P_{cpu}     &= \\lfloor (C - R) / c \\rfloor \\\\\n"
"P_{license} &= \\lfloor L / T(c) \\rfloor \\\\\n"
"P_{actual}  &= \\max(1, \\min(P_{req}, P_{license}))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:152
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"where *C* = physical cores, *R* = reserved cores (default 1), *c* = cores"
" per job, and *L* = available tokens. :math:`P_{cpu}` is computed only to"
" decide whether to emit the CPU-oversubscription warning — it no longer "
"bounds :math:`P_{actual}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:157
msgid "Use :func:`~ABQflow.plan_parallelism` to compute this directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:160
msgid "Fault Tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:162
msgid ""
"``run_batch`` uses :class:`concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor` with "
"these guarantees:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:165
msgid ""
"**Single-job isolation**: an exception in one worker returns as an error "
":class:`~ABQflow.JobOutcome` — it does not kill the batch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:167
msgid ""
"**Clean process lifecycle**: the executor context-manager guarantees "
"worker cleanup on completion or error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:169
msgid ""
"**Pickle-safe workers**: the top-level ``_worker`` function (not a lambda"
" or closure) is the entry point, ensuring Windows ``spawn`` "
"compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:173
msgid "JSON Protocol"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:175
msgid ""
"Hook scripts and monolithic scripts communicate results via stdout.  The "
"framework uses a **sentinel-marker** approach to reliably extract JSON "
"even when Abaqus prints banner text or warnings to stdout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:188
msgid ""
"When sentinel markers are absent, the framework falls back to scanning "
"from the **end** of stdout for the last complete JSON object (Abaqus "
"banner precedes script output, so the last ``{`` is most likely the "
"result)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:192
msgid ""
"``ABQflow.hookkit`` (staged into the job's working directory "
"automatically) implements this protocol for hook scripts so authors never"
" write sentinel markers or argparse plumbing by hand. It is single-file "
"and stdlib-only — never imports ``ABQflow``, ``odbAccess``, ``abaqus``, "
"or ``numpy`` — so it runs unmodified under the Abaqus Python interpreter "
"(Py2.7 or Py3). It also adds a field-output mode (``hookkit.field()``) "
"that spills large result sets (>10k rows or >1MB) to a CSV sidecar "
"instead of inlining them in the JSON payload, keeping stdout small for "
"bulky field quantities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/architecture/architecture.rst:201
msgid ""
"``ABQflow.datkit`` is staged the same way, but only for hooks with "
"``source='dat'``, and carries the same contract — single file, stdlib "
"only, Py2.7 and Py3, never imports ``ABQflow``.  It parses Abaqus's "
"printed tables (``N O D E`` / ``E L E M E N T`` / ``E N E R G Y`` / ``C O"
" N T A C T`` output) into rows and columns, streaming so that "
"``parse(path, increments='last')`` costs one increment however long the "
"analysis ran. ``test/unit/test_hookkit_py27.py`` enforces the Python 2.7 "
"promise on both files with an AST scan."
msgstr ""

